module.exports = "# MajuLaw Estate Administration \u2014 Assistant Knowledge Base\n\n> **Purpose of this file.** This is the *only* source of information the site's\n> assistant is permitted to answer from. It contains vetted, public-facing\n> guidance on administering a Muslim estate in Singapore (Faraid, the\n> Inheritance Certificate, the Public Trustee, and Letters of Administration).\n> It is drawn from the MajuLaw research file with all internal project material\n> removed (timeline, meeting notes, staff names, budget and vendor quotes).\n>\n> **Not legal advice.** Everything here is general information. It is not a\n> substitute for advice from the Syariah Court, MUIS, the Family Justice Courts,\n> the Public Trustee, or a qualified lawyer.\n>\n> **Review before use.** Confirm every figure, fee, timeframe, and legal\n> statement is current and accurate before this file is used to answer real\n> users. The assistant will repeat whatever is written here.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The Inheritance Certificate (Syariah Court)\n\n### 1.1 Who is eligible to apply\n\nTo apply for any inheritance-related certificate, the applicant must belong to\n**one** of the following categories:\n\n- **A beneficiary** \u2014 must be a Muslim and at least 21 years old at the time of application.\n- **A law firm** \u2014 applying on behalf of a beneficiary.\n- **MUIS** \u2014 applying on behalf of Baitulmal.\n- **A court or authority** \u2014 under a duty to determine the persons entitled to\n  share in the estate of a deceased, or the shares to which they are entitled.\n\n### 1.2 Applying for a new Inheritance Certificate\n\n**Information and documents required**\n\n- **Death records:** Death Certificate, extract of the death certificate, an\n  Order of Court of presumption of death, or a Certificate of presumption of\n  death of the deceased.\n- **Identification (Singpass):** the applicant's Identification Card (IC) or a\n  valid passport.\n- **Proof of relationship:** documents proving the applicant beneficiary's\n  relationship to the deceased.\n- **Legal personal representative:** must provide lawful appointment by an Order\n  of Court or legal instrument. A Statutory Declaration from the beneficiary\n  consenting to the application may also be required.\n- **Other beneficiaries:** relevant legal documents (e.g. a will) proving the\n  claim. If documents cannot be provided, an affirmed Statutory Declaration is\n  required. Non-Faraid beneficiaries must remove their name from the Annex to the\n  Statutory Declaration before submitting.\n- **Beneficiary details:** full names and NRIC/ID numbers of all lawful potential\n  beneficiaries.\n- **Discrepancy explanation:** documents explaining any inconsistencies (e.g.\n  spelling differences in names). Without documentary proof, an affirmed\n  Statutory Declaration confirming the information is required.\n- **Authorisation letters (if applicable):**\n  - *Law firms:* a letter showing express instructions to apply and the purpose of the certificate.\n  - *MUIS / Court / Authority:* a letter showing the officer is authorised to apply.\n- **Translations:** any documents in Jawi script or in languages other than\n  English/Malay must be accompanied by a certified true English translation.\n\n**Application process**\n\n1. **Submission** \u2014 submit online via the SYC Portal using SingPass (individuals)\n   or CorpPass (entities). Foreign individuals without SingPass must apply for an\n   SYCPass.\n2. **Processing and validity** \u2014 applications are processed within 7 days and are\n   valid for 60 days.\n3. **Rectifications** \u2014 if marked \"Incomplete\", the applicant must amend and\n   re-submit within 7 days. All steps must be completed within the 60-day\n   validity period, or the application expires.\n4. **Statutory Declaration (SD)** \u2014 once approved, download the SD template from\n   the SYC Portal dashboard, complete it, affirm it before a Commissioner for\n   Oaths, and upload it back to the portal for verification.\n5. **Issuance** \u2014 after the SD is verified and payment is made, the Inheritance\n   Certificate is issued as a downloadable PDF.\n   - *Note:* payment must be made at least 1 day before the 60-day validity\n     expires. Certificates issued after 23 July 2025 have no download time limit.\n\n**Fees:** SGD $34.00 (payable via PayNow, eNETS, or credit card).\n\n### 1.3 Extract of an Inheritance Certificate\n\nAn extract can be applied for if an Inheritance Certificate was previously issued.\n\n- **Information required:** the Inheritance Certificate number of the previously\n  issued certificate and/or the NRIC/ID number of the deceased.\n- **Process:** submit online via the SYC Portal (SingPass, CorpPass, or SYCPass);\n  processed within 7 working days; valid for 60 days. The extract is available as\n  a downloadable PDF upon payment, which must be made at least 1 day before the\n  60-day validity expires.\n\n### 1.4 Revision of an Inheritance Certificate\n\nA revision applies **only** where one or more of the beneficiaries stated in the\noriginal certificate dies **before the final distribution** of the estate.\n\n- *Note:* if a revision is needed due to typographical errors or an omitted\n  beneficiary, a **brand new application** must be submitted instead.\n- **Documents required:** the original Inheritance Certificate number and/or the\n  deceased's NRIC/ID number; the Death Certificate of the deceased beneficiary;\n  the full name and NRIC/ID of the deceased beneficiary's own beneficiaries;\n  proof of relationship to the deceased beneficiary; certified English\n  translations for Jawi or non-English/Malay documents.\n- **Process:** submit online via the SYC Portal; processed within 7 days; valid\n  for 60 days; incomplete applications must be rectified within 7 days. Once\n  approved, download, affirm the SD before a Commissioner for Oaths, and upload\n  it. After SD verification and payment, the revised certificate is issued as a\n  PDF (payment at least 1 day before the 60-day validity expires).\n- **Fees:** SGD $34.00 per page (payable via PayNow, eNETS, or credit card).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Faraid \u2014 administering a Muslim estate (eight-step framework)\n\nAdministering a Muslim estate in Singapore follows an eight-step framework that\nintegrates Shari'ah requirements with civil probate law:\n\n1. **Settle immediate post-demise claims** in strict legal sequence: funeral and\n   burial expenses; commercial debts owed to creditors; and outstanding religious\n   liabilities such as unpaid Zakat, Kafarah (expiation payments), or oath\n   penalties.\n2. **Register and obtain the official Death Certificate** through the National\n   Environment Agency (NEA), the hospital, or the Ministry of Home Affairs (MHA).\n3. **Apply to the Syariah Court** for an official Inheritance Certificate, which\n   identifies all legal Faraid heirs and specifies their exact fractional shares.\n4. **Conduct a full search and inventory** of all estate assets across\n   institutions including HDB, banks, insurance companies, the Public Trustee's\n   Office (IPTO), and investment entities.\n5. **Appoint a probate lawyer** to petition the Family Justice Courts for formal\n   legal authority \u2014 a Grant of Probate if a valid Wasiat exists, or a Grant of\n   Letters of Administration if the deceased died intestate \u2014 executing any\n   necessary Renunciation documents or Deeds of Undertaking.\n6. **Liquidate assets into cash** \u2014 the administrator or executor presents the\n   court order and Inheritance Certificate to financial institutions and property\n   agents.\n7. **Disburse net estate funds** in strict sequence: Faraid shares to heirs per\n   the Inheritance Certificate; Wasiat bequests (up to 1/3 to non-heirs); Nuzriah\n   obligations; and any unallocated surplus remitted to Baitulmal.\n8. **Appeals** \u2014 if disputes arise over Baitulmal remittances, beneficiaries may\n   submit a formal appeal to MUIS and the Syariah Court.\n\n### 2.1 Special scenarios and frequently asked questions\n\n- **Adopted children** \u2014 not blood relatives and do not inherit automatically\n  under Faraid. Adoptive parents can provide for them by making a Wasiat leaving\n  up to 1/3 of their net estate to the adopted child, or Faraid heirs may\n  voluntarily agree post-demise to gift them a portion.\n- **Reverts and non-Muslim family members** \u2014 non-Muslims cannot inherit\n  automatically as Faraid heirs from a deceased Muslim. However, a Muslim revert\n  can prepare a Wasiat to bequeath up to 1/3 of their net estate to non-Muslim\n  family members.\n- **Civil marriage with a non-Muslim spouse** \u2014 a non-Muslim spouse married in a\n  civil ceremony is not recognised as a lawful spouse under Islamic law and cannot\n  inherit as a Faraid beneficiary.\n- **Children born out of wedlock** \u2014 children born outside a valid Islamic\n  marriage do not inherit from their biological father under Faraid, but retain\n  full inheritance rights from their mother and her side of the family.\n- **Equal distribution via mutual consent** \u2014 while Faraid is the default legal\n  framework, heirs may mutually agree to divide the estate equally or alter share\n  ratios, provided all affected heirs are above 18 years of age and give informed\n  consent via a Deed of Family Undertaking.\n- **Un-nominated CPF monies** \u2014 if no CPF nomination is made, savings are\n  transferred to the Public Trustee's Office (PTO / IPTO). Legal heirs must apply\n  online at www.ipto.gov.sg or present documentation to the Public Trustee for\n  disbursement in accordance with the Syariah Court Inheritance Certificate.\n\n---\n\n## 3. The Public Trustee\n\n### 3.1 What is a Public Trustee?\n\nA Public Trustee is a government-appointed office that acts as an independent\nadministrator for certain financial and estate matters. In Singapore, the Public\nTrustee's Office (PTO) primarily administers eligible small deceased estates,\nmanages certain compensation and protected funds, and distributes assets to\nrightful beneficiaries when no private executor or administrator is available.\n\nFor deceased estates, the PTO can only administer estates that meet its\neligibility requirements and fall within the prescribed value limits. If an estate\n**exceeds the PTO's limits (typically under $50,000)**, the family must apply to\nthe Singapore Courts for a Grant of Probate (where there is a valid will) or\nLetters of Administration (where there is no will). Where the estate is worth more\nthan $50,000, the PTO cannot administer it and the family or next-of-kin must\nformally apply to the Singapore Courts for a legal grant.\n\n*Reference: Section 3, Public Trustee Act 1915.*\n\n### 3.2 Passing away without a will\n\n**Non-Muslim:** the estate is generally distributed according to the Intestate\nSuccession Act. If it qualifies (typically under $50,000 and excluding CPF\nmonies), the Public Trustee may administer it. Common first steps:\n\n1. Obtain the death certificate.\n2. Gather information on assets and liabilities.\n3. Determine the value of the estate.\n4. If it qualifies (typically under $50,000), submit an application to the Public\n   Trustee using Singpass, with supporting documents (birth certificates,\n   marriage certificates, identity documents, etc.).\n5. The Public Trustee identifies the beneficiaries and distributes the estate\n   according to law.\n\n**Muslim:** if the deceased was Muslim and died without a will, the estate is\ndistributed according to Muslim inheritance law (Faraid). The Public Trustee does\nnot decide the beneficiaries itself (Administration of Muslim Law Act (AMLA),\ns 112). Common first steps:\n\n1. Obtain the death certificate.\n2. Apply to the Syariah Court for an Inheritance Certificate, which identifies the\n   legal beneficiaries and their respective shares.\n3. Submit the Inheritance Certificate and supporting documents to the Public\n   Trustee (where PTO administration applies).\n4. The Public Trustee distributes the estate according to the shares stated in the\n   Inheritance Certificate.\n\n### 3.3 Letters of Administration vs. the Public Trustee\n\n**Letters of Administration**\n\n- A court order issued by the Family Justice Courts.\n- Required where there is no valid will and PTO administration is unavailable.\n- The administrator obtains authority to collect and distribute assets.\n\n**Public Trustee**\n\n- A government agency.\n- Suitable only for qualifying estates.\n- Distributes the estate according to the applicable law.\n- Generally simpler and less costly for eligible estates.\n\n### 3.4 How the Public Trustee distributes an estate\n\n- **Non-Muslim:** according to the Intestate Succession Act. For example \u2014 spouse\n  only: spouse receives the estate; spouse and children: share according to the\n  Act; children only: share equally.\n- **Muslim:** according to the Inheritance Certificate issued by the Syariah Court\n  under Faraid principles.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Letters of Administration (LOA) \u2014 procedure at the Family Justice Courts\n\nGenerally, where the estate's total value (excluding CPF and nominated insurance)\n**exceeds $50,000**, the Next-of-Kin (NOK) must apply to the Family Justice Courts\n(FJC) to obtain Letters of Administration to distribute and manage the deceased's\nassets.\n\n- Estates valued **at or below $5 million** \u2014 apply to the **Family Court**.\n- Estates valued **above $5 million** \u2014 apply to the **Family Division of the High\n  Court**.\n\n### 4.1 Optional caveat search\n\nBefore applying, it is optional to conduct a search of the court's record of\nprobate cases and caveats filed in relation to the deceased's estate. Benefits:\n\n- It helps avoid situations where an application might be rejected \u2014 for example,\n  if a grant of Letters of Administration has already been issued for the same\n  estate.\n- It can reveal pending cases or caveats in force against the estate. If so, the\n  court may be prevented from issuing the LOA and independent legal advice may be\n  needed.\n  - *A caveat is a document filed by someone with a legal interest in the estate.\n    The court is required by law to give the person who filed the caveat the\n    opportunity to contest any application for a grant.*\n\nTo conduct a caveat search, visit the Service Bureau with the deceased's\nidentification number. The search should be done on the day you file the LOA\napplication, for the present year.\n\n### 4.2 Filing on or after 15 October 2024\n\n**Stage 1 \u2014 Making the application**\n\n| # | Document | Notes |\n|---|----------|-------|\n| 1 | **Originating Application for Probate (Form 162)** | Formal document filed to initiate obtaining legal authority to manage and distribute the estate. Filing must be done within 6 months of the deceased's date of death. Book an appointment at the Service Bureau, which uses the information you provide about the deceased and the estate to generate the Originating Application. |\n| 2 | **Schedule of Assets (Form 177)** | A detailed inventory of properties, bank accounts, investments, and secured liabilities owned at death. The court uses it to confirm the estate value and determine the administration bond in certain circumstances. Contact the relevant institutions (e.g. banks, HDB) to obtain the information. |\n| 3 | **Copy of the Inheritance Certificate (Syariah Court)** | Mandatory supporting document for Muslim estates. When filing, the applicant must legally declare the rightful beneficiaries and the exact size of their shares; for Muslim estates this is governed by Faraid, and the Syariah Court Inheritance Certificate is the only document the FJC accepts as proof of these shares. Obtain it from the Syariah Court **before** the originating application. |\n\n**Optional documents at Stage 1**\n\n| # | Document | When applicable |\n|---|----------|-----------------|\n| 4 | **Renunciation of other beneficiaries (Form 166)** | Only if other beneficiaries have given up their right to apply for LOA. Those renouncing must affirm the renunciation before a Commissioner for Oaths. |\n| 5 | **Certified true copy of the deceased's death certificate** | Only if the deceased was not issued a digitally verifiable death certificate by the ICA Registry of Births and Deaths. Deaths on/after 29 May 2022 receive a digital death certificate (downloadable from My Legacy). For deaths before that, apply for a digital death extract from ICA, or have a hardcopy certified by a Commissioner for Oaths. |\n| 6 | **Copy of the death certificate of other next-of-kin** | To prove that relatives with an equal or prior right to apply are no longer alive. Same digital/hardcopy rules as above. |\n| 7 | **Copy of the deceased's divorce certificate** | Only if the deceased obtained a divorce during their lifetime, to prove the ex-spouse is no longer entitled to inherit. Apply for an extract via the SYC Portal by filing a Praecipe (Request) in E-Services with the required payment. |\n\n**Stage 2 \u2014 Filing of supporting documents**\n\n| # | Document | Notes |\n|---|----------|-------|\n| 8 | **Supporting Affidavit (Form 163)** | Affirms that the originating application, schedule of assets, and other supporting documents are true. Must be filed within 14 days after the originating application. If the Schedule of Assets was already filed, include the averment in paragraph 2 of the Sample; if not, omit the averment and later file a Supplementary Affidavit (see #10). Extensions of time can be requested at the Service Bureau with reasons and supporting documents. |\n| 9 | **Administration Oath (Form 167)** | A separate sworn statement promising to faithfully administer and account for the estate. Must be filed within 14 days after the originating application. |\n| 10 | **Supplementary Affidavit** | Confirms the Schedule of Assets is true and accurate. Only required if the Schedule of Assets is filed after the initial application and Supporting Affidavit. |\n\n**Stage 3 \u2014 Attend hearing (if required)**\n\n- *Scenario A:* the court schedules a hearing after the LOA application is filed.\n  Attendance is compulsory if supporting documents were not filed or not accepted\n  before the hearing date. Non-attendance may result in dismissal. If approved,\n  the LOA is issued (proceed to Stage 4); otherwise, there is an option to appeal\n  (Stage 5). Hearings may be virtual or in person.\n- *Scenario B:* if all documents are accepted before the hearing date, the court\n  may approve the application and notify you by letter that attendance is not\n  required (proceed to Stage 4).\n\n**Stage 4 \u2014 Extracting the LOA**\n\n| # | Document | Notes |\n|---|----------|-------|\n| 11 | **Request for Certified True Copy of Documents** | After approval, the court issues the LOA. Some organisations require a certified true copy of the LOA or Schedule of Assets before releasing funds. File a Request for Certified True Copy at the Service Bureau, stating the reason and specifying the documents needed. |\n\n**Stage 5 \u2014 Appeal (optional)**\n\n| # | Document | Notes |\n|---|----------|-------|\n| 12 | **Notice of Appeal (Form 136)** | If unsatisfied with the outcome or the contents of the LOA, file within 14 days of the decision or order. Independent legal advice is recommended for an appeal. |\n\n### 4.3 Filing before 15 October 2024\n\nThe process is the same, but the forms differ (and there are no Stage 3\ndocuments):\n\n- Stage 1: Originating Summons and Statement for Letters of Administration;\n  Schedule of Assets (**Form 226**); Copy of the Inheritance Certificate (same as\n  after 15 Oct 2024).\n- Optional at Stage 1: Renunciation of other beneficiaries (**Form 53**);\n  certified true copy of the death certificate; death certificate of other\n  next-of-kin; deceased's divorce certificate (all same as after 15 Oct 2024).\n- Stage 2: Supporting Affidavit (**Form 225**); Administration Oath (**Form 54**);\n  Supplementary Affidavit (same as after 15 Oct 2024).\n- Stage 4: Request for Certified True Copy of Documents (same as after 15 Oct\n  2024).\n- Stage 5: Notice of Appeal (**Form 165**, Family Justice Court Practice\n  Directions 2015, Appendix A).\n\n---\n\n## 5. The law governing Letters of Administration\n\nThe Family Justice Courts' power to grant Letters of Administration for an\nintestate estate comes from **s 18 of the Probate and Administration Act 1934\n(PAA)**. For a Muslim domiciled in Singapore, the identity and shares of the\npersons beneficially entitled to the net intestate estate are governed by Muslim\nlaw (as modified where applicable by Malay custom) under **s 112(1)\u2013(2) of the\nAdministration of Muslim Law Act 1966 (AMLA)**. The Intestate Succession Act 1967\n(ISA) is expressly inapplicable to every Muslim estate (ISA s 2).\n\n### 5.1 Who may apply for LOA?\n\nSection 18 of the PAA determines whether and to whom LOA is granted. The general\norder of priority for entitlement to apply is:\n\n> Spouse \u2192 Children \u2192 Parents \u2192 Siblings \u2192 Nephews/Nieces \u2192 Grandparents \u2192 Uncles/Aunts.\n\nThe administrator must be at least 21 years old, not lacking mental capacity, and\nbe a beneficiary under Faraid.\n\n### 5.2 Must an Inheritance Certificate be obtained before LOA is issued?\n\nNo substantive Singapore statute expressly makes the certificate a condition\nprecedent to the FJC's power to grant LOA. PAA s 18 grants the power; AMLA s 113\nrequires a Mazhab statement; AMLA s 115 permissively authorises a certificate of\n\"opinion\".\n\nIn practice, the certificate is ordinarily the authoritative evidence needed to\nidentify heirs, assess competing rights to administration, and direct Faraid\ndistribution. It is **not conclusive** of facts, title, or estate composition, and\ncan be disregarded or replaced when based on wrong facts. Nevertheless, it is one\nof the required supporting documents for an LOA application at the FJC.\n\n### 5.3 What does the LOA cover?\n\nIt covers beneficial property and enforceable rights forming the deceased's\nestate, subject to the grant's terms. On grant, interim Public Trustee vesting\nceases and estate property vests in the administrator for administration. The\ngrant does not itself prove that every scheduled item belongs to the estate (PAA\ns 37). After collection, funeral/administration expenses, debts, and liabilities\nare paid; only the **net** estate is distributed under AMLA s 112 and the reliable\ncertificate.\n\n### 5.4 Which assets are included in or excluded from the LOA estate?\n\n| Category | In LOA estate? | How it passes | Authority |\n|----------|----------------|---------------|-----------|\n| Validly nominated CPF monies | **Excluded** | Statutory trust; deemed non-estate and immune from the deceased's debts. CPF Board pays/transfers to the nominee in their own right; a minor nominee's portion may be held/paid via the Public Trustee. | CPF Act ss 24(3A), 25; *Saniah*; Fatwa on CPF nomination |\n| Un-nominated CPF monies | **Excluded** | Still statutorily non-estate. CPF Board pays the Public Trustee, who determines Muslim-law beneficiaries and distributes under the statutory scheme, ordinarily using the Inheritance Certificate. | CPF Act s 25A; Public Trustee guidance |\n| Insurance trust nomination | **Excluded** | Insurance Act s 132 creates a present statutory trust; s 132(4) excludes policy monies from the estate/debts. Insurer pays trustee/nominees; trustee protects minor nominees. | Insurance Act s 132 |\n| Insurance revocable nomination | **Excluded** | Statutory beneficiary designation under s 133; the death benefit is payable to the nominee, not collected under LOA. | Insurance Act s 133; Nomination Regulations; Fatwa on revocable insurance nomination |\n| No effective insurance nomination | **Included** | No statutory nominee/trust exclusion merely from holding a policy. If payable to personal representatives/estate, the administrator collects and applies Faraid distribution only after estate debts are cleared. | Insurance Act ss 134 onward |\n| Registered joint-tenancy land | **Excluded** | The deceased's interest is extinguished at death by survivorship; the survivor becomes sole owner by operation of law. No Faraid distribution of the extinguished interest. | *Shafeeg*; MUIS 2019 joint-tenancy fatwa |\n| Tenancy-in-common land | **Included** | The deceased retains an undivided beneficial share; the administrator administers that share and distributes the net value under AMLA s 112. | *Shafeeg*; MUIS 2019 fatwa |\n| Joint bank account | **Unclear** | No categorical Singapore Muslim-estate rule found; case law is silent. *Shafeeg*'s land holding does not automatically apply to joint accounts. | *Shafeeg* |\n| Property held by the deceased as bare trustee | **Excluded** | The deceased lacked beneficial interest; the successor trustee/limited administrator deals only with legal title; trust beneficiaries remain beneficial owners. | PAA s 23; *Mahidon* |\n| Wakaf | **Excluded** | Special AMLA statutory administration and vesting; MUIS administers and the property vests in MUIS. | AMLA ss 58\u201359; *Syed Abbas* |\n| Valid completed inter vivos hibah/gift | **Excluded** | Beneficial ownership left the deceased during life; the donee owns. The civil court determines validity under Muslim and general law. | *Mohamed Ismail*; *Haja Maideen* |\n| Purported death-bed/testamentary device (Nuzriah) | **Included unless legally valid** | Under AMLA s 60(2), all Nuzriah made after 1 July 1968 is void unless exceptions apply. An invalid or excess disposition falls into the estate and is distributed under Faraid. | AMLA ss 60, 111; *Mohamed Ismail* |\n\n### 5.5 How are minors and vulnerable heirs protected?\n\nAMLA s 112 fixes share entitlements under Faraid. The Guardianship of Infants Act\n(GIA), Trustees Act, PAA, and Mental Capacity Act regulate who may receive, hold,\napply, or dispose of property. No Singapore authority permits the administrator to\nreduce, postpone, or redirect a minor's Faraid share merely because another\nallocation seems fairer. A minor or person lacking capacity cannot consent to\nrenounce or dispose of their own shares.\n\nAn administrator should retain a minor's share on an identified trust, segregate\nand prudently invest it, keep beneficiary-specific accounts, and use only\napplicable statutory or express maintenance/advancement powers. Trustees Act s 33\nauthorises reasonable income for maintenance, education, or benefit; s 34\nauthorises advancement of capital within limits.\n\nA parent or guardian is not automatically entitled to take capital beneficially.\nGIA ss 16\u201317 restrict disposition and deny a guardian an effective discharge for\ncapital. Court leave, payment to an appropriate trustee/Public Trustee, or\nretention by the administrator may be necessary depending on the asset and\ngoverning trust (GIA ss 15\u201318).\n\n### 5.6 Court supervision and fiduciary enforcement\n\nAvailable safeguards include:\n\n- Refusing a grant to an infant or a person lacking capacity, and appointing a\n  guardian/trustee instead (PAA ss 21\u201322).\n- An administration bond under PAA s 29 \u2014 security for the \"due administration of\n  the estate\". It is typically given by the administrator with two sureties who\n  co-sign and financially back the administrator's performance. The bond's value\n  is usually the total sworn value of the estate within the jurisdiction,\n  calculated without deducting debts (except those secured by a mortgage).\n- The welfare of the minor as the court's paramount consideration in any\n  proceedings concerning a minor's property, with security/leave requirements\n  under GIA ss 3, 15\u201317.\n- A beneficiary's right to demand a full account of the administration, with\n  supporting documentation proportionate to value and complexity (*Chen Xiaoqi*).\n- Recovery of trust property from an administrator, subject to equitable defences\n  (*Mahidon*).\n- Revocation or amendment of the LOA for sufficient cause (PAA s 32).\n\nThe Inheritance (Family Provision) Act safety net is unavailable because Muslim\nestates are expressly excluded (IFPA s 1(2)).\n\n### 5.7 Essential additional advice\n\n1. Prove Singapore domicile and Mazhab (school of law) before assuming AMLA s 112.\n2. Decide estate composition first before asking whether distribution by Faraid\n   attaches.\n3. Verify the Inheritance Certificate's factual inputs; obtain correction where\n   family composition is incomplete.\n4. Investigate debts and statutory liabilities before any Faraid distribution.\n   Funeral and administration expenses, debts, and statutory liabilities are\n   discharged from estate assets before succession (PAA s 57 and Second Schedule).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Legal authorities (reference)\n\n**Legislation**\n\n- Probate and Administration Act 1934 \u2014 grant, vesting, limited grants, security, accounts, debts, supervision (ss 2, 18, 20\u201324, 28\u201332, 37, 54\u201358, 66; Second Schedule).\n- Administration of Muslim Law Act 1966 \u2014 Muslim distribution, will restrictions, Mazhab, proof and certificate (ss 32, 58\u201360, 110\u2013119, especially 111\u2013115).\n- Intestate Succession Act 1967 \u2014 express exclusion of Muslim estates (s 2).\n- Central Provident Fund Act 1953 \u2014 statutory trust and non-estate CPF distribution (ss 24(3A), 25, 25A).\n- Insurance Act 1966 \u2014 trust and revocable nominations, payment of death benefits (ss 132\u2013134).\n- Trustees Act 1967 \u2014 personal representative as trustee; maintenance and advancement (ss 33\u201334).\n- Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 \u2014 welfare, security, and limits on dealings with a minor's property (ss 3, 15\u201318).\n- Inheritance (Family Provision) Act 1966 \u2014 expressly excludes Muslim estates (s 1(2)).\n- Civil Law Act 1909 \u2014 survival of causes of action; uncertain order of death (ss 10, 30).\n- Wills Act 1838 \u2014 relevant to partial intestacy and invalid wills (formal provisions preserved by AMLA s 111(2)(a), except s 3).\n\n**Cases**\n\n- *Shafeeg bin Salim Talib v Fatimah bte Abud bin Talib* [2010] SGCA 11 (Court of Appeal; binding) \u2014 estate composition precedes Faraid; joint-tenancy land excluded; private fatwa not binding; joint personal property left open.\n- *Saniah bte Ali v Abdullah bin Ali* [1990] 1 SLR(R) 555 (High Court) \u2014 CPF statutory trust excludes monies from the Muslim estate.\n- *Re Will of Shaik Ahmad bin Abdullah Wahdain Basharahil* [2002] SGHC 165 \u2014 the civil court finds facts; the Inheritance Certificate is opinion on found/hypothetical facts.\n- *Mohamed Ismail bin Ibrahim v Mohammad Taha bin Ibrahim* [2004] SGHC 210 \u2014 one-third/heir restrictions; invalid Nuzriah; the certificate fixes shares.\n- *Mahidon Nichiar bte Mohd Ali v Dawood Sultan Kamaldin* [2015] SGCA 36 (Court of Appeal; binding) \u2014 the certificate depends on supplied facts; the administrator is a trustee; informed renunciation and conflicts.\n- *Haja Maideen v Roshan Begum* [2017] SGHC 164 \u2014 hibah subject to mandatory HDB law; the certificate does not determine asset title.\n- *Syed Abbas v MUIS* [2009] SGHC 281 \u2014 Wakaf administered and vested under AMLA; the Trustees Act cannot displace MUIS.\n- *Chen Xiaoqi v Chen Fangqi* [2023] SGHC 107 \u2014 the administrator's fiduciary/accounting duties; the schedule is not conclusive; remuneration.\n- *AAG v Estate of AAH, deceased* [2009] SGCA 56 (Court of Appeal; binding) \u2014 confirms the IFPA's express exclusion of Muslim estates.\n\n**Other materials**\n\n- MUIS Joint Tenancy 2019 fatwa \u2014 official religious opinion; non-binding in civil court; recognises survivorship consistently with *Shafeeg*.\n- MUIS Revocable Insurance Nomination fatwa \u2014 treats nomination as hibah; civil effect rests on the Insurance Act.\n- MUIS CPF Nomination fatwa \u2014 official religious guidance; statutory civil effect governed by the CPF Act and *Saniah*.\n- \"Muslim Law of Inheritance in Singapore: Principles and Practice\" [2025] SAL Prac 22 \u2014 recent professionally reviewed commentary; useful on institutional practice, not binding.\n- Public Trustee's Office CPF guidance \u2014 official administrative explanation of nominated and un-nominated CPF payment channels.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Glossary\n\n- **IC / IHC** \u2014 Inheritance Certificate (issued by the Syariah Court).\n- **SYC** \u2014 Syariah Court.\n- **LOA** \u2014 Letters of Administration.\n- **FJC** \u2014 Family Justice Courts.\n- **CPF** \u2014 Central Provident Fund.\n- **MUIS** \u2014 Islamic Religious Council of Singapore.\n- **Faraid** \u2014 Islamic law of inheritance.\n- **Wasiat** \u2014 Islamic will (may direct up to 1/3 of the net estate to non-Faraid beneficiaries).\n- **Nuzriah** \u2014 a death-bed/testamentary device (void after 1 July 1968 unless exceptions apply).\n- **Baitulmal** \u2014 the Muslim community fund administered by MUIS.\n- **PTO / IPTO** \u2014 Public Trustee's Office.\n- **NOK** \u2014 Next-of-Kin.\n- **SD** \u2014 Statutory Declaration.\n";
